-- title:
--   Frobenius acts as q T_q on the monodromy toric part of J₀(Nq)
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ and $q$ be nonzero natural numbers with $q$ prime and $q \nmid N$. Assume [`ModularCurve.HeckeInputsAll (N * q)`](def/ModularCurve_HeckeInputsAll.html#L8), i.e. that the input predicate `HeckeInputsAlong` over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ holds at level $Nq$ for every prime $\ell$, and assume [`ModularCurve.HeckeOperatorsCommuteBar (N * q)`](def/ModularCurve_HeckeModule.html#L25), i.e. that the operators `heckeOperatorBar` for all primes commute pairwise as endomorphisms of $J =$ [`ModularCurve.JZero (N * q)`](def/ModularCurve_ArithmeticGalois.html#L115), the group of degree-zero divisor classes $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the base-changed modular function field of level $Nq$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$. Let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $q$ a nonunit of $A$, and let $\mathrm{frob}$ be a $\mathbb Q$-algebra automorphism of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lying in the decomposition subgroup of $A$ over $\mathbb Q$ and acting on the residue field of $A$ by $x \mapsto x^{q}$. Equip $J$ with the `HeckeAlg`-module structure `heckeModuleBar (N * q)`, where `HeckeAlg` $= \mathbb Z[X_\ell : \ell \text{ prime}]$ acts through the commuting operators `heckeOperatorBar` (the commutation hypothesis selects this branch of the definition). Let $\mathcal T$ be the `HeckeAlg`-submodule of $J$ spanned by the elements $\sigma \cdot x - x$ with $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of $A$ over $\mathbb Q$ (viewed inside the Galois group) and $x \in J$ annihilated by some positive integer coprime to $q$. The conclusion, [`ModularCurve.ToricFrobeniusHecke`](def/ModularCurve_ToricDescentData.html#L86), is that for every $x \in \mathcal T$ one has $\mathrm{frob} \cdot x = (q \cdot X_q) \cdot x$, i.e. Frobenius acts on $\mathcal T$ as $q$ times the Hecke generator at $q$.
--
--   This is the Frobenius–Hecke relation of Ribet's geometric analysis of $J_0(Nq)$ at the prime $q$ of multiplicative reduction, with the toric part realised concretely as the monodromy submodule generated by inertia differences on torsion of order coprime to $q$; classically $\mathrm{Frob}_q = q\,U_q$ on the toric points. It feeds the toric dichotomy for $J_0(Nq)$, the bound on the rank of the monodromy toric part, and from there the level-lowering input to the Fermat argument.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_toricFrobeniusHecke_toricMonodromyPart_jZero.lean

